<BaseLayout title="Sources | How Ancient China Worked" description="How this project chooses, checks, and presents sources about imperial China.">
  <section class="page-intro shell narrow"><p class="eyebrow">Sources</p><h1>How we use sources</h1><div class="prose"><p>Articles draw first on academic books and peer-reviewed research, then on primary sources in established translations. We identify interpretive uncertainty rather than hiding it.</p><h2>Useful starting points</h2><ul><li>Patricia Buckley Ebrey, <em>The Inner Quarters</em>.</li><li>Mark Edward Lewis, <em>The Early Chinese Empires</em>.</li><li>The <em>Cambridge History of China</em> series.</li></ul><h2>Reading historical names</h2><p>Names are especially difficult because records may be incomplete, posthumous, or shaped by status and genre. We explain the source type wherever it matters to the answer.</p></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
